  "preamble": "The Middle Chinese aspirated alveolo-palatal affricate [tɕʰ] (the 昌 initial) is reflected as the aspirated postalveolar affricate [tʃʰ] in Hailu Hakka (spoken in Taiwan, including Zhudong). The change shifts the constriction slightly back and lowers the tongue body while keeping aspiration. Hailu thereby keeps the 章 series apart from the 莊 and 精 series, which both give alveolar /tsʰ/ (初 /tsʰu/), whereas Sixian and Meixian Hakka merged everything into /tsʰ/.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Depalatalization, Retraction",
